{ Today we learn how to finish games. First the ladder mate: two rooks take turns giving check, and every check pushes his king one row closer to his back row, the edge of the board. [%csl Ge1,Gf5] }
44... Rh3 { He slides his rook to the edge column to threaten mate on your back row, because his other rook already guards your second row. But he forgot his own king. Check him first, with the rook on your back row. [%cal Rh3h1,Ye1e6] } 45. Re6+ { Check along the row his king stands on. Your other rook guards the row next to it, closer to you, so his king can only climb towards his back row. [%cal Ge6a6,Gf5a5] } (45. Rf6+ { Also a check, but this is the rook that guarded the row next to his king. That row is open now, and his king slips back towards the middle. [%csl Rb5] } 45... Kb5 46. Re5+ Ka4 47. Kg1 { His king is out of the ladder, and you had to spend a move guarding your back row instead of checking. The mate is gone and the game is even. [%csl Ra4] }) (45. Kg1 { Your king steps next to the corner to guard it, but that hands him the move, and now his rook does the checking. } 45... Rg3+ 46. Kf1 Rf3+ 47. Kg1 Rg3+ { His rook checks your king again and again, first on one column and then the next, and your king can never get away. That is a draw. [%csl Rg1] }) (45. Rxa5+ { A pawn with check, but your rook lands right beside his king, and no piece of yours protects that rook. } 45... Kxa5 { His king eats your rook. You are a rook down now, and his threat of mate on your back row is still there. [%csl Ra5] }) (45. Re7 { A rook move with no check, and that gives him time. His own ladder is already built. } 45... Rh1# { Mate, but on your own king! His rook checks along your back row while his other rook guards your second row. Every move had to be a check. [%csl Rf1] }) 45... Kb7 { His king climbs one row. Now the other rook takes its turn, with a check on his new row. Keep checking: one move without a check and his rook mates you from the corner. [%cal Yf5f7,Rh3h1] } 46. Rf7+ { Check again, and the rooks have swapped jobs: this one checks, the other guards the row he just left. One more climb and his king is on his back row. [%cal Gf7a7,Ge6a6] } (46. Re7+ { Also a check, but this is the rook that guarded the row he just left. That row is open again, and his king steps back towards the middle. [%csl Rc6] } 46... Kc6 47. Rf6+ Kb5 48. Re5+ Ka4 49. Kg1 { His king walked right out of the ladder, and your last move had to guard your own king instead of checking his. The win is gone. [%csl Ra4] }) (46. Rb6+ { That check lands right next to his king, and nothing of yours guards the rook there. } 46... Kxb6 { His king takes your rook for free. A whole rook down, and his threat against your back row is still on the board. [%csl Rb6] }) (46. Kg1 { You stop to guard the corner, and he uses the free move to bring his rook back to defend. } 46... Rc3 47. Rf7+ Rc7 48. Rxc7+ Kxc7 { His rook came back to block the check, a pair of rooks came off, and one rook cannot build a ladder. Nobody is better now. [%csl Rc7] }) (46. Rb5+ { A check along his column, but it pushes his king sideways instead of onto his back row, and he still threatens mate in your corner. } 46... Kc7 47. Re2 Rh1+ 48. Kg2 Rxe2+ 49. Kxh1 { A pair of rooks came off while you stopped his mate, and his king got away from the ladder. It is an even game now. [%csl Rc7] }) 46... Kc8 { His king has reached his back row, the edge of the board. Leave the rook on the row next to his king where it is, and bring your other rook to his back row with check. [%cal Ye6e8] } 47. Re8# { Mate! One rook checks along his back row and the other guards the row next to it, so his king has no square. That is the ladder mate, and his own mate never came. [%cal Ge8a8,Gf7a7] } (47. Rf8+ { A check from the wrong rook: this one was guarding the row next to his king, and now his king steps back off his back row. [%csl Rd7] } 47... Kd7 48. Re2 Rh1+ 49. Kg2 Rxe2+ 50. Kxh1 { One pair of rooks came off while you stopped his mate, and his king walked free. The mate is gone and the game is level. [%csl Rd7] }) (47. Rc7+ { Your rook lands right next to his king, and no piece of yours guards the rook there. } 47... Kxc7 { His king eats the rook, and the mate on his back row is gone with it. You are a rook down, and he still threatens mate in your corner. [%csl Rc7] }) (47. Rc6+ { A check along his column, but his king just steps along his back row. } 47... Kb8 48. Rb6+ Kc8 { Your checks only chase his king back and forth, and he always has a square to go to. It is a draw. [%csl Rc8] }) (47. Kg1 { Your king steps next to the corner to guard it, one move before the mate, and his rook gets time to defend. } 47... Rg3+ 48. Kf1 Rd3 { His rook checked you and then came back to guard his back row. The mate is gone, and nobody is winning. [%csl Rd3] }) 1-0


[Event "Basic checkmates: ladder, take the blocker"]
[Result "0-1"]
