[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/4r3/3k1p2/5P1R/2n3R1/8/1r6/7K/8_w_-_-_1_40"]

{ You are Black, and his rook is attacking your knight. His king stands alone near the corner, and you have two rooks. The ladder again: can you find it? }
40. Rxc5 { He takes your knight and thinks he has won a piece. That is his mistake: he forgot about his own king. } 40... Re2+ { Check along his second row! Your other rook guards his third row, so his king has to go back to his back row, the edge of the board. [%cal Ge2h2,Gb3h3] } (40... Rb2+ { Also a check, but this is the rook that guarded his third row. That row is open now, and his king climbs out towards the middle. [%csl Rg3] } 41. Kg3 Rg8+ 42. Kf4 { His king has walked out towards the middle, and his own two rooks are free to fight back. The ladder is gone and the game is level. [%csl Rf4] }) (40... Rh3+ { A check, but that square is guarded by his rook on the edge column, and by his king too. [%cal Rh6h3] } 41. Rxh3 { His rook takes yours for free. A whole rook down, and the ladder is gone with it. [%csl Rh3] }) (40... Re1 { Your rook reaches his back row, but it gives no check, so he gets a free move to defend. } 41. Rc2 { His rook now guards his second row, and your ladder has nowhere to start. Nobody is better now. [%csl Rc2] }) 41. Kg1 { His king steps back to his back row, the edge of the board. } 41... Rb1+ { Check along his back row, while your other rook keeps guarding his second row. His king has no square to go to. [%cal Gb1g1,Ge2h2] } (41... Re1+ { Also a check, but this is the rook that guarded his second row. That row is open now, and his king steps off the edge and attacks your rook. [%csl Rf2] } 42. Kf2 { His king escaped from the edge and is hitting your rook. The ladder has fallen apart, and the game is even. [%csl Re1] }) (41... Rg2+ { A check right next to his king, but nothing of yours guards that rook. } 42. Kxg2 { His king eats your rook. A rook down, and no ladder left. [%csl Rg2] }) (41... Rbb2 { Both your rooks on his second row look scary, but neither gives check, and that hands him a free move. } 42. Rf5 { His rook comes back to guard the column next to his king, so a check on his back row can now be blocked. The game is level. [%csl Rf5] }) (41... Rg3+ { A check, but along his column instead of his back row, so his king just steps into the corner. } 42. Kh1 Re1+ 43. Kh2 { Your rooks cannot take turns on his back row from here, and his king steps back out of the corner. The mate is gone. [%csl Rh2] }) 42. Rc1 { He throws his rook in the way of the check. } 42... Rxc1# { Mate! You take the blocker and the check is back, while your other rook still guards his second row. That is the ladder mate, and your knight was a small price for it. [%cal Gc1g1,Ge2h2] } (42... Re1+ { The other rook checks instead, and his rook simply takes yours. } 43. Rxe1 Rxe1+ 44. Kf2 { One pair of rooks came off, and his king walked out towards the middle. The mate is gone. [%csl Rf2] }) (42... Rg2+ { A check right beside his king, with nothing guarding your rook. } 43. Kxg2 Rxc1 { You won his rook back, but now it is one rook each, his king is free, and nobody is better. [%csl Rg2] }) 0-1


