{ You are Black, and his queen and pawn are closing in on your king. Your own queen is far away in a corner. It looks bad. }
39. d6 { His pawn attacks your bishop, and now he threatens mate: his queen checks, then takes your bishop right beside your king. He was winning, and he forgot about his own king. } 39... Rg1+ { Check! Your queen guards the rook from the far corner, so his king cannot take it. His king has only one square to run to. [%cal Ga1g1] } (39... Bxd6 { You take the pawn, but his queen can take your bishop back, with check. } 40. Qxd6+ { Your bishop is gone and his queen checks your king, with nothing of yours nearby to help. He is winning. [%csl Rd8] }) (39... Qg7+ { A check from your queen, but his rook can step in the way and attack her. } 40. Rg3 { His rook blocked the check and hits your queen, and you have no more checks that help. You are lost. [%csl Rg3] }) (39... Bb6 { Your bishop steps away from the pawn, but that does nothing about his threats, and his queen checks at once. } 40. Qe7+ Kc8 41. d7+ { His pawn checks your king and is one step from a new queen. He is winning easily. [%csl Rd8] }) (39... Rb2 { A rook move with no check, and that loses here: his queen strikes first. } 40. Qe7+ Kc8 41. Qxc7# { Mate, but on your own king: his queen landed right beside it, guarded by his pawn. Every move had to be a check. [%csl Rc8] }) (39... Bb8 { Your bishop steps back beside your king, but his queen checks at once. } 40. Qe7+ Kc8 41. d7+ { His queen and pawn have your king trapped against the edge, and there is no defence left. [%csl Rc8] }) 40. Kh3 { His king runs to the edge column, the only square it had. } 40... Qh8+ { Your queen races the whole length of the long diagonal and checks along the edge column, while your rook guards the column next to it. [%cal Ga1h8,Gg1g4] } (40... Rg3+ { Another rook check, but his rook can take it. } 41. Rxg3 { His rook took yours, his mate threat is still waiting, and the game is lost. [%csl Rg3] }) (40... Qf1+ { Your queen checks along the diagonal, but his rook stands right next to that square and takes her. [%cal Rf2f1] } 41. Rxf1 { A whole queen gone, and his threats against your king are still there. [%csl Rf1] }) (40... Bxd6 { You grab the pawn, but that is no check, and his queen takes your bishop with check. } 41. Qxd6+ Kc8 42. Qc6+ { His queen chases your king into the open, your pieces are too far away to help, and his mate is close. [%csl Rc8] }) 41. Qh6 { He throws his queen in the way. It is the only thing that stops the check. } 41... Qxh6# { Mate! Your queen takes his queen and the check is back, while your rook guards the column next to his king. A queen and a rook make a ladder too. [%cal Gh6h3,Gg1g4] } (41... Rg3+ { Another check, but it lets his king take your rook, and the net around his king falls apart. } 42. Kxg3 Qxh6 43. dxc7+ Kxc7 { You won his queen, but his king took your rook and his pawn took your bishop, and his two rooks are worth more than your queen now. He is better. [%csl Rg3] }) 0-1


