{ He has five pawns against your one, and he is sure the game is his. }
42... Bh4 { His bishop attacks your rook, and he is sure he is winning it. That is his mistake: he has not looked at his own king. } 43. Rd2+ { Check along the column his king stands on, while your other rook guards the column next to it. His king has to step one column further away. [%cal Gd2d7,Ge1e7] } (43. Rd1+ { Also a check, but your other rook is still under attack, and his king slips in behind his pawns. [%cal Rh4f2] } 43... Kc8 44. Ra2 { Your rook had to run from the bishop, and his king is tucked in safely behind his pawns. He is much better. [%csl Rc8] }) (43. Ra2 { You save the rook, but it was shielding your other rook from the bishop, and now his bishop can take that one. [%cal Rh4e1] } 43... Bxe1 44. Kxe1 Rxf3 { His bishop swapped itself for a rook, and his rook took your last pawn. He is winning. [%csl Rf3] }) (43. Re7+ { A check, but his bishop is watching that square and takes your rook. [%cal Rh4e7] } 43... Bxe7 { A whole rook gone, and one rook alone cannot build a ladder. [%csl Re7] }) (43. Rh2 { Your rook attacks his bishop, but that rook was blocking the bishop's path to your other rook. [%cal Rh4e1] } 43... Bxe1 44. Kxe1 Rxf3 { Your back rook is gone, his rook took your pawn, and he has five pawns against none. You are lost. [%csl Rf3] }) 43... Kc6 { His king steps over to the next column. } 44. Rc1+ { Check again, from the other rook, while the first one guards the column he just left. [%cal Gc1c6,Gd2d6] } (44. Rc2+ { Also a check, but this is the rook that guarded the column he just left, so his king steps back into that column. } 44... Kd5 45. Rd2+ Kc4 { His king walked back into the middle, and your ladder has fallen apart. The game is level. [%csl Rc4] }) (44. Re6+ { A check along his row, and his king simply steps out of it. } 44... Kc5 45. Re5+ Kb6 { His king is safe beside his pawns and your ladder is broken. With five pawns against your one, he is better. [%csl Rb6] }) (44. Rd6+ { That check lands right next to his king, and nothing guards your rook there. } 44... Kxd6 { His king takes your rook for free, and one rook cannot finish the ladder. [%csl Rd6] }) 44... Kb5 { His king steps over one more column, closer to the edge. } 45. Rb2+ { Another check with the other rook. One more column, and his king runs out of board. [%cal Gb2b5,Gc1c5] } (45. Rb1+ { This check comes from the rook that was holding the column he left, and his king slips right back into it. } 45... Kc4 { His king slipped back towards the middle, and the ladder is broken. Nobody is better now. [%csl Rc4] }) (45. Rc5+ { That check lands right beside his king, with nothing guarding your rook. } 45... Kxc5 { His king takes the rook for free, and now he is winning. [%csl Rc5] }) (45. Rd5+ { A check along his row, but his king just steps out of it. } 45... Ka6 { His king hides beside his pawns, you have no good checks left, and he is much better. [%csl Ra6] }) 45... Ka4 { His king has reached the edge column. There are no columns left to run to. } 46. Ra1# { Mate! One rook checks along the edge column and the other guards the column next to it. Four checks, four rungs: a long ladder, and his bishop never got your rook. [%cal Ga1a4,Gb2b4] } (46. Ra2+ { Also a check, but this is the rook that guarded the next column, and his king slips out through it. } 46... Kb3 { His king escaped from the edge. The mate is gone and the game is level. [%csl Rb3] }) (46. Rb4+ { That check lands right next to his king, and nothing guards your rook. } 46... Kxb4 { His king eats your rook when you were one move from mate, and now he is winning. [%csl Rb4] }) (46. Rc4+ { A check along his row, but his king slides along the edge column and out of the net. } 46... Ka3 { His king slipped away along the edge, and the mate is gone. Now the game is even. [%csl Ra3] }) (46. Rxb7 { A free pawn, but mate was one move away, and his bishop uses the time to guard the corner. } 46... Bf6 47. Rxa7+ Kb5 { His bishop guards the corner square, and his king walks out of the net. The mate is gone and the game is about even. [%cal Rf6a1] }) 1-0


