{ Now the back rank mate. His own pawns fill the squares in front of his king and do the job of a second rook, so a rook check on his back row that he cannot block is mate. [%csl Rg8,Rf7,Rg7,Rh7] } 37... Rxd1 { He grabs your rook, and that is fine. Your other rook now runs all the way up the empty column to his back row. [%cal Yc1c8] } 38. Rc8+ { Check. His king cannot step up, because his own pawns fill every square in front of him, so he has to block. [%csl Rf7,Rg7,Rh7] } (38. Rxd1 { Taking the rook back is the greedy move. The rook was never the prize here, the mate was, and you have just let it go. } 38... Re2+ 39. Kc1 g6 { His pawn steps up and leaves the red square free for his king, so the mate is gone and you are a knight and a pawn down. [%csl Rg7] }) (38. Rc7 { The seventh row looks strong, but his knight guards that square and eats your rook for nothing. [%cal Rb5c7] } 38... Nxc7 39. Kc2 Rg1 { His knight has taken your last rook. No rook, no mate, and it is two rooks and a knight against your four pawns. [%csl Rc7] }) 38... Rd8 { He throws his rook in the way. Take it, because every blocker just comes off and his king still cannot leave the back row. [%cal Yc8d8] } 39. Rxd8+ { Nice, one blocker gone and it is check again. He still has a second rook to throw in front of his king. [%csl Re3] } (39. Rc1 { You back off with the rook and give up his back row. The mate was yours, and now his two rooks come for your king. } 39... Rd2+ 40. Rc2 Ree2 { His two rooks trap yours right beside your king. You have to give it up, and then his rooks and knight mate you. [%csl Rb2] }) 39... Re8 { That is the last piece he has to throw in the way. Take it, and his king has nothing left to hide behind. [%cal Yd8e8] } 40. Rxe8# { Mate! His own pawns kept him at home and your rook owns his back row. That is the back rank mate. [%csl Rg8,Rf7,Rg7,Rh7] } (40. Rd7 { Mate was one move away and your rook goes pawn hunting instead. His back row is free again. } 40... Rb8 41. Rd5 Nc7 { His rook slipped off the back row and his knight is safe, so there is no mate left and he is a knight and a pawn up. }) (40. Rd1 { Mate was sitting on the board and your rook goes home. That hands him a free move to look after his king. } 40... h6 41. Kc2 Re2+ { His pawn step gave his king air, and now his rook is checking your king while he is a knight and a pawn up. }) 1-0


