{ You are Black, and his queen is about to take yours. Winning her straight back looks like the only move on the board. It is not the best one. } 16. Qxb4 { His queen snaps up yours, and he is already sure the game is his. } Rd1+ { Check on his back row, and you never take his queen back. Whatever he throws in the way you take, and the check comes again. } (16... Bxb4 { You win the queen back and the game is level again. The mate you walked past was worth far more than a queen. } 17. Rd3 b5 18. Nc3 Bc5 { An ordinary even game, two rooks each and nothing to attack. A forced mate was sitting in your hands. }) (16... O-O { You tuck your king away safely, and he helps himself to your bishop as well. The attack was the point here. } 17. Qxc5 Rd1+ 18. Qg1 Rxg1+ 19. Kxg1 { Your rook trades itself for his queen, and even then you are a rook and a bishop down for nothing at all. }) (16... Rd4 { The rook looks busy on that square and attacks nothing. It hands him a free move and your bishop with it. } 17. Qxc5 Rd1+ 18. Qg1 Rxg1+ 19. Kxg1 O-O { He is a rook and a bishop up, his king is castled and safe, and the mate you had is long gone. }) 17. Qe1 { He drags his queen all the way home to block the check on his own back row. } Rxe1+ { Take the queen, with check again. His king still has nowhere to step, so he has to block one more time. } (17... Rxc1 { You take the bishop instead of the queen, and his rook takes yours straight back. The mate is gone. } 18. Rxc1 b6 19. Qd2 f5 { His queen is free and busy, your king is still stuck in the middle, and you are a queen and a rook down for one bishop. }) (17... Rd8 { Your rook retreats with mate one move away, and his knight collects your bishop on the way. } 18. Nxc5 O-O 19. Nd3 { He has your queen and your bishop, and his knight sits happily in the centre. Nothing is left to attack with. }) 18. Rf1 { His last rook drops in front of the check. That is the final blocker on his back row. } Rxf1# { Mate! Take the last blocker and his king is stuck behind his own two pawns. That is a back rank mate, and your queen paid for it. [%csl Rh1,Rg2,Rh2] } (18... Bb4 { A fine square for the bishop, but mate was one move away and this lets his rook take your rook. } 19. Rxe1 Bxe1 20. Be3 b5 { Everything has been traded off and the material is dead level. A mate in one turned into a long ordinary game. }) (18... Bf2 { The bishop lands on a lovely square while mate was there for the taking, and his rook takes your rook. } 19. Rxe1 Bxe1 20. Be3 Nc8 { Material is level, his king has the whole board to walk about in, and there is no mate anywhere. }) 0-1


