{ His rook is aiming at your queen and he is already counting up what he wins. He has not looked at his own king once. Mate in two. } 21... Rxd2 { He takes your queen and sits back, a whole queen up and certain the game is over. } 22. Bxe6+ { Your bishop grabs a pawn with check, and the only square his king can reach is the corner. } (22. Rd1 { You attack his rook and he simply takes yours. His king was the thing to go after, never that rook. } 22... Rxd1 23. Rxd1 Qf2 { The rooks come off, his queen lands right beside your king, and you are a queen for a rook down with no mate anywhere. }) (22. Rf8+ { A rook check looks brave, but nothing of yours is guarding that square, so his king simply eats the rook. } 22... Kxf8 23. c5 Qf2 24. Bxe6 Qxe3 { His king took a whole rook for free and strolled to safety. He is a queen up and your attack is over. }) (22. Bf3 { You save the bishop from his queen and his pawn simply takes it anyway. The mate was worth far more than a bishop. } 22... exf3 23. Rf2 Rxf2 24. Kh2 Qg5 { That pawn has crawled in beside your king, and with his queen and rook joining in it is your king being mated, not his. }) 22... Kh8 { His king hides in the corner, with his own two pawns packed in beside him. Now finish him off. } 23. Rf8# { Mate! Your rook drops onto his empty back row, his own pawns hold him in, and your bishop covers his last square. That is a back rank mate. [%csl Rh8,Rg7,Rh7][%cal Ge6g8] } (23. Rf7 { Your rook goes pawn hunting and the mate on his back row is gone. One move was all it needed. } 23... h5 24. g3 Qg5 25. Rf5 Qxf5 { His queen mops up your rook, he is a whole queen up, and your attack has completely run out. }) (23. Bg8 { A cute idea, but the bishop stands there undefended and his king happily takes it. } 23... Kxg8 24. Rf4 Qg3 25. Rg4 Qxe3 { His king stands safe in the open, your bishop is gone for nothing, and he has a queen, a rook and a bishop against two rooks. }) (23. Rf4 { You chase his queen instead of mating him, and your rook leaves the row where the mate was. } 23... Qe7 24. d5 g5 25. Rf5 Bc6 { His queen is tucked away safely, his bishop defends her, and he is a queen up with nothing to fear. }) 1-0


