{ You are Black, with a queen against his rook, knight and two pawns. It looks like his game. }
83. a5 { He pushes his pawn towards a new queen, and he has forgotten his own king. That is his mistake. } 83... Qe8 { Your queen pins his knight to his king, so the knight cannot move. Now you threaten mate, and his pieces are almost out of moves. [%cal Ge8a8] } (83... Qxb7+ { A pawn with check, but his rook is standing right beside it and takes your queen. } 84. Rxb7+ { His rook took your queen, with check. He is winning easily now. [%csl Rb7] }) (83... Qd5 { Your queen pins his pawn, but his knight can jump out with check. [%cal Gd5a8] } 84. Na6+ { His knight escaped with check and is free to chase your queen away from his pawn. He is winning. [%csl Rb8] }) (83... Kd6 { Your king steps onto an open row, and his rook checks you at once. } 84. Ra6+ { His rook is free and checking you, and only his own knight stands in front of his pawn. You are lost. [%csl Ra6] }) (83... Qc6 { Your queen pins his pawn from close up, but his knight can take her there. } 84. Nxc6 { His knight took your queen, and with no queen left, the game is his. [%csl Rc6] }) 84. a6 { His pawn steps up. Look at his pieces now: not one of them can move. } 84... Qxb8# { Mate! Your queen takes the knight beside his king, guarded by your king: the king and queen mate. A move without a check could easily have been stalemate. [%cal Gc7b8] } (84... Qd8 { Stalemate! He is not in check and none of his pieces can move, so the game ends in a draw. [%csl Ra8] }) (84... Kd6 { Your king steps away, and now he has no legal move and is not in check. That is stalemate, a draw. [%csl Ra8] }) (84... Qf8 { Your queen slides along the row his king stands on and keeps the pin, but it gives no check, and he has no move at all. Stalemate: a draw. [%csl Ra8] }) (84... Qg8 { The pin still holds, but this is no check, and nothing of his can move. That is stalemate, and the game is drawn. [%csl Ra8] }) (84... Qh8 { Your queen goes all the way to the corner, still pinning the knight, and leaves him without a single move. Stalemate, a draw. [%csl Ra8] }) (84... Kb6 { Your king steps next to his pawn, but that is no check, and none of his pieces can move. Stalemate: the game is a draw. [%csl Ra8] }) (84... Qc8 { Your queen steps right next to his knight, but his pawn can take her there. } 85. bxc8=Q+ Kxc8 { You got his new queen back, but your own queen is gone, and his rook and knight are too much for your bare king. [%csl Rc8] }) 0-1


