{ His own rook, bishop and pawns are crammed in around his king, leaving him exactly one free square. There is a mate in two here. } 13. Nc3 { He develops his knight in the middle of the board and never once looks at his own king. Your turn. } Qh1+ { Your queen dives into the corner beside his king. Nothing of his can take her and his king cannot move, so a piece has to block. } (13... Qh3 { Your queen lands right next to his pawn, and pawns capture diagonally, so he simply eats her. } 14. gxh3 Nh2+ 15. Nxh2 Bxh2 16. Qb3 { You gave a queen and got one pawn back, and the knights came off as well. He is winning easily now. }) (13... Nxf2 { You throw your knight at his pawns with nothing behind it. His king just takes and stays perfectly safe. } 14. Kxf2 Qg6 15. Rh1 c5 16. Kg1 { A knight given for one pawn, and his king has tucked itself back into the corner. There is no attack left. }) (13... Bh2 { Your bishop slides to the corner where it does nothing at all, and that free move lets him trade off the pieces you needed. } 14. Ne5 Bxe5 15. Bxg4 Qh1+ 16. Ke2 { The knights and bishops have come off and his king walks out into the open. Your mate needed those pieces. }) 14. Ng1 { He has to bring his knight all the way home to block, and that fills the last free square beside his king. } Nh2# { Mate! Your knight gives the check and your queen holds it in place, while five of his own pieces block every escape. That is a smothered mate. [%csl Rf1,Ge1,Ge2,Gf2,Gg1,Gg2] } (14... Bh2 { Right square, wrong piece: the bishop gives no check there. His bishop takes your knight and your queen is left stranded. } 15. Bxg4 Qxg1+ 16. Ke2 Qxg2 17. Bf3 { His bishop attacks your queen and your bishop is loose as well. A mate in one has turned into a scramble to survive. }) (14... Nxf2 { The other knight jumps in, but nothing is guarding it and his king simply takes it. } 15. Kxf2 Qh4+ 16. Kf1 Nd7 { You gave a knight for one pawn and his king stepped back to safety. The mate that was there has gone. }) (14... Nh6 { Your knight retreats from the one square that mates, and he gets a free move to untangle his pieces. } 15. Bf3 Nd7 16. Ne4 { He brings a knight across to guard and his king can breathe again. One careless retreat handed the whole win back. }) 0-1


