{ His knight is about to snatch your queen, so counting material will not save you here. Find the mate in two instead. } 24. Nxc7 { He grabs your queen and is sure the game is over. But look at his king: it has one free square and nothing else. } Re1+ { Your rook checks along his back row. Only his bishop can get in the way, and blocking fills his king's last free square. } (24... Nf2+ { You check with the knight one move too early, and his bishop is guarding that square, so he just takes the knight. } 25. Bxf2 Re5 26. Bd4 Rf8 27. Bxe5+ { Your queen, a knight and a rook are all gone and he is still checking you. He is the one winning now. }) (24... Rac8 { You chase his knight instead of going after his king, which hands him a free move to start his own attack. } 25. Bd4+ Re5 26. Qe6+ Kg5 27. Qxe5+ { His bishop and queen have hunted your king into the open and swept up a rook. The mate you had is a memory. }) (24... bxc5 { You take his bishop, but that bishop was never the point, and his rook scoops up your knight on the back row instead. } 25. Rxd1 g5 26. Rf1+ Ke5 27. Qg3+ { His rook and queen are chasing your king across the board and you are the one about to be mated. }) 25. Bg1 { His bishop has to come all the way home to block, and now his own bishop and pawns seal every square around his king. } Nf2# { Mate! The bishop he brought home to save himself is the wall that traps him, and your rook guards the knight. That is a smothered mate. [%csl Rh1,Gg1,Gg2,Gh2] } (25... Rxg1+ { You take the bishop with check, but that bishop was the wall holding his king in. His king takes back and is free. } 26. Kxg1 Nf2 27. Qe6+ Kg5 28. h4+ { From mate in one to a lost game: your rook is gone, his queen is checking, and his pawn is joining in too. }) (25... Rc8 { A quiet rook move with mate sitting there for the taking. Now he has a free move and your king is the open one. } 26. Qh4+ Kf5 27. g4+ Kg6 { His queen and his pawn are driving your king into the open, and it is you being hunted now. }) 0-1
